  "comparisonProfiles": [
    {
      "id": "semantic-jpg-mutate-v1",
      "description": "JFIF 1.01 JPEG mutation round trips. The LOSSY half — `lumaHistogram`, an 8-bucket luma census — is the only numeric member, and the only one the tolerance below is for: the reference `image` codec and this subset's own from-scratch codec are two independently written implementations, so a real photographic fixture re-encoded through each lands luma mass in the same coarse buckets but not identical per-bucket counts. Raw samples are never reported: this platform's comparison tolerance is per-number and absolute with no aggregate mode, so an unbounded sample array could never be compared honestly. Every EXACT member — `dimensions`, `jfifVersion`, `jfifDensity`, each retained segment's marker/length/payload digest, and each DQT payload digest — is spelled as a STRING on purpose, because the comparison engine applies `tolerance` to numbers and compares strings by equality: reported as numbers under a 400000 slack, the real 2275x2560 scan and a 3x2 stub compared EQUAL, and so did every JFIF header field. The slack is sized against the ~5.8 megapixel real-world fixture this case reads (shared://🖼️abbau-aufbau-masterarbeit-grundriss.jpg): measured, one decode/re-encode at quality 90 moves at most 2018 pixels between buckets, a pass through quality 50 at most 10014 and through quality 5 at most 55570 — so it still fails genuine corruption (blank, inverted or solid output) while double-JPEG rounding noise between the two codecs does not, and it is why set-re-encode-quality is observed through the DQT payloads rather than through the histogram. quantTables is excluded from the identity round trip's own comparison (ignoreKeys is not used for it; the case's handler names it), because BOTH codecs regenerate the DQT from re_encode_quality over the Annex K.1 base tables rather than carrying the source's forward.",
      "tolerance": 400000,
      "ignoreKeys": []
    }
  ],
